Title = {Credible Sustainability Signals, Consumer Trust, and Purchase Intention
   in Selected Middle Eastern Online Retail Contexts},
Journal = {SAGE OPEN},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {16},
Number = {3},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {This research aims to explore the extent to which sustainable e-commerce
   practices are perceived as credible signals amid digital uncertainty
   and, in turn, affect consumer responses. The current research fills an
   important research gap regarding sustainable e-commerce practices and
   their impact on trust, purchase intention, and willingness to pay a
   premium over time, specifically within selected e-commerce settings in
   the Middle East. The current research adopted a two-wave panel data
   approach to analyze data from 600 active e-commerce users in Saudi
   Arabia, Jordan, and the West Bank using PLS-SEM. The current research
   found significant effects of sustainable e-commerce practices on trust
   and purchase intention, and that trust is a significant yet moderate
   mediator between sustainability perceptions and subsequent purchase
   intention. The current research also found that environmental concern
   does not have significant moderating effects on the core model, and
   willingness to pay a premium does not have significant short-term
   effects. Theoretically, this research contributes to sustainable
   e-commerce practices by highlighting credibility-based digital cues,
   rather than positive attributes, as an important yet incomplete mediator
   between sustainability perceptions and consumer response. In practice,
   this research has implications for e-commerce managers and
   practitioners, indicating that they are more likely to benefit from
   verifiable sustainability communications than from environmental
   rhetoric, particularly in the short term for building trust and
   utilization.},
